WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
kaar

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.840
Summer in the polar regions and the sun never sets.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:51.080
Imagine a single day that lasts for months.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:59.720
Some polar animals will face great challenges as their ice world vanishes around them.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.720
Animals must use this special time wisely, for summer's riches will not last.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:41.720
The summer is just beginning and the increasing power of the sun is creating a spectacular new landscape.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:57.720
This melting ice sheet in the Arctic is 150 miles long with a thousand waterfalls.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:09.720
Vast expanses of ice that were once stone still have come to life.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:47.720
The polar bear's world is melting away under the constant summer sun.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:03:06.720
As the season advances, the frozen surface of the ocean disintegrates. Soon the ice here will have totally vanished.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:18.720
The polar bear family have to adapt to their rapidly changing home.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:33.720
For the two cubs, this is a whole new watery world. It's the first time they've seen the sea ice break up.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:43.720
They're only six months old and will be dependent on their mother for another two years.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:54.720
They have much to learn.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:11.720
Their first swimming lesson and they're not sure that they want it.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:17.720
For their mother, swimming is second nature.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:34.720
But the cubs still prefer to have ice beneath their feet whenever they can.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:43.720
Soon it will be impossible to avoid the water.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:55.720
This lone male has lived through a dozen summers and is perfectly at home in the sea.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:15.720
He can swim up to 50 miles a day. He's hungry and he's searching this ice maze for seals.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:36.720
But traveling across this increasingly fragile ice scape is hard work for an animal weighing over half a ton.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:06:05.720
It's also harder to hunt when you have to swim. In open water, the odds are in the seals' favor.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:19.720
Lean months lie ahead for the polar bears. They will have to adopt new hunting strategies or risk starvation.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:37.720
The northern part of the earth is now tilting towards the sun and all around the Arctic, the sea ice continues its retreat.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:07:03.720
The last remnants of the ice world drift with the wind and the tides.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:30.720
The sun's heat may be gentle at these latitudes, but it is continuous for 24 hours a day and it carves the ice into magical shapes.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:40.720
Those that need ice have to visit the glacier fronts to find it.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:53.720
A comfortable bed is hard to come by.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:08:04.720
A polar bear's fur is so dense that water is easily shaken off.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:29.720
And ice absorbs water like a towel.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:34.720
That's better.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:45.720
There's now little chance of catching seals and this bear may not eat again until the end of summer.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:59.720
It's better to save energy and doze in the sun.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:27.720
The warmth of the sun's rays is now bringing the Arctic lands back to life.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:43.720
So that Jewel is going to lieutenant Tim Thomas stand on the mountain as he feeds up some more.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:10:07.120
Red phalloropes.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:13.240
They've flown all the way from the tropics to feed in these rich arctic waters.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:25.360
They stir up the tiny creatures that are now flourishing here with a special spinning dance.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:33.760
All excellent fuel as they hurry to rear the next generation.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.200
The Arctic terns have made an even longer journey to breed here.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:44.920
They have flown 11,000 miles from the Antarctic.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:49.760
Their newly hatched chicks will need to go fast if they're to accompany their parents

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:57.160
when they return south in only six weeks' time.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:03.200
An eiderduck has chosen to nest in the center of the tern colony, hardly a tranquil place

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.880
to raise your young.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:27.720
But the noisy neighbors have a feisty attitude to life, and that can be very valuable.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:34.480
A hungry bear looking for a meal can destroy hundreds of nests in a single raid.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:45.840
Defensive squadrons of terns take off immediately.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.480
Sharp beaks stab down from above.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:12:08.560
The bear has nothing in his armory that can cope with this.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:21.400
The terns have drawn blood from his muzzle.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:28.720
He leaves in search of an easier meal.

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:39.960
The eiders survive thanks to their choice of neighbors.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:47.800
And the ducklings begin their sprint to maturity.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:52.760
Fishing continues around the clock as the terns race to rear their young before the

00:12:52.760 --> 00:13:04.560
return of the freeze.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:10.920
So the rich waters of summer fuel the breeding of all these visitors and enable them to build

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:28.160
up the reserves they will need for the long return journey south.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:41.640
In land the winter snows have gone revealing a vast treeless wilderness.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:51.520
The male snowy owl is finding plenty of lemmings to bring to his mate.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:14:01.760
But they're not just for her.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:06.960
She is brooding their rather scruffy young.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:12.360
She tears the meal into beak sized chunks that the owlets can swallow whole.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:25.720
Each of them can eat two lemmings a day.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.880
The male has no time to rest.

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:32.720
Over the course of the summer he will have to provide his family with over a thousand

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:37.320
lemmings.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:44.720
The hungry owlets keep the adults working around the clock.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:50.840
The Arctic summer may be short but the days are long.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:54.360
Everyone must take advantage of the 24 hour daylight.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:15:14.360
None more so than the owls tiny neighbours the Lapland buntings.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:28.720
Both parents feed their growing family continuously.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:38.480
A mere ten days after hatching and they will all have left in record time.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:49.960
The last needs a little encouragement.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:56.200
The owlets have also left their nest and turned into football sized balls of fluff.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:06.320
But they still depend on their parents for food and protection.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:09.000
A skewer.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:35.240
Their mother's talons are her best weapons.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:40.440
The owlets are dangerously exposed out on the tundra so they must hurry to change from

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.920
balls of fluff to fully feathered adults.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:57.240
And to do that they need more lemmings.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:09.920
A lemming doesn't last long these days.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:29.520
Wings are harder for the wolves here in the high Arctic.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:45.840
The cubs are now six weeks old and increasingly hungry.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:55.440
The adults have to struggle to feed their growing family.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:13.160
Somewhere on this vast expanse of tundra there must be larger prey.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:23.040
Musk oxen are on the move.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:40.360
They're heading into the valleys where the brief summer rains will produce fresh grazing.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:53.640
This is an opportunity that must be seized even if it means travelling 80 miles in a

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:55.640
day.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:19:00.440
Their task is a formidable one.

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:15.120
Musk oxen are immensely powerful and their sharp horns can kill.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:20.000
A heavily armoured bull would be an unwise choice.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:31.280
Even two wolves would find it a struggle to bring it down.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:56.960
A calf, much easier.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:22.580
The two wolves work together to split the herd and isolate their victim.

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:31.400
It seems that the wolf cubs will at last eat well.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:41.840
But the herd regroups.

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:49.600
The cavalry ride to the rescue.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:59.160
The whole herd encircles the calf with a protective wall of horns.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:17.480
For the Musk oxen it's all for one and one for all.

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:26.160
For the wolves another attack would be not only futile but dangerous.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:45.340
They have spent a lot of energy and have nothing whatever to show for it.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:22:02.840
The failure will be felt most keenly back at the den.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:26.360
They have nothing to take back to the family.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:36.880
The pack are forced to move on in search of better hunting.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:45.100
They must find something soon in this vast wilderness to feed their growing family.

00:22:45.100 --> 00:23:07.620
The brief Arctic summer is almost over.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:13.580
At the southern end of the planet the long summer days transform life just as they do

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:15.140
in the north.

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:25.940
But the cast here is very different.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.220
King penguins.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:47.620
A parent returning with food must recognize its chicks call amongst a chorus of 400,000

00:23:47.620 --> 00:24:01.020
penguins.

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:10.900
Huge colonies like this one are found all along the north coast of South Georgia.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:17.060
King penguins are active throughout the long summer days so they have to deal with an uncharacteristic

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:28.420
polar problem.

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:38.240
By midday the temperature can reach a sizzling 17 degrees centigrade.

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:41.440
Any effort can lead to overheating.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:47.880
It's best not to overexert oneself.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:25:06.500
The heavily insulated penguins stretch out so their naked feet can cool in the breeze.

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:10.220
Their gigantic neighbors use a different approach.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:26.500
White sand cools the backs of these elephant seals and also acts as a sunscreen.

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:41.700
Their chicks in their downy coats that have kept them warm all winter are in even greater

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:50.260
danger of overheating.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:55.300
It will be two months before they can swim properly but a dip in the shallow river brings

00:25:55.300 --> 00:26:15.100
a little relief.

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:23.380
There is however another way to cool the blood.

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:35.500
This murky pool has become a penguin spa.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:40.100
It's a great way to cool the feet but there is no reason to stop there.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:58.660
You can indulge yourself with the full treatment.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:14.980
Mild, glorious mud.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:25.460
For the fully-fettered adults there's a cleaner, more invigorating option.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:42.020
The bracing waters of the Southern Ocean.

00:27:42.020 --> 00:28:03.300
baw objection

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:31.820
The

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:38.340
hundred mile long island of South Georgia lies on the northern fringe of Antarctica.

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:53.620
Ice-free all year, its rich coastal waters make it a popular breeding destination for all beach

00:28:53.620 --> 00:29:12.980
lovers. In summer, 95% of the world's population of Antarctic fur seals come here. Packed tightly

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:23.380
together, they form one of the densest gatherings of marine mammals on earth. The island's beaches

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:32.300
are filling fast and will soon be crammed with five million of these summer visitors. Space is

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:38.860
at a premium as every female needs a dry patch of sand the size of a beach towel for herself.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:59.300
And for good reason. Virtually all of them give birth within just 10 days.

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:18.540
It's crucial to establish a strong bond with your baby in such a crowded colony.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:44.460
The pups grow quickly on rich high fat milk. After giving birth, the females are ready to mate,

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:51.780
so each male guards up to 15 of them in his small patch of beach. A bull may only hold the

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:59.740
territory for one season in his entire life. So when a challenger arrives, he will risk

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:14.740
everything to retain it.

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:57.100
Their sharp teeth inflict terrible injuries. Many bulls die from exhaustion after these fights.

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:06.540
In the heat of the battle, the pups are also in real danger.

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:30.300
The defeated bull makes his escape, but the colony still suffers from the side effects of the battle.

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:43.060
Many of the pups get lost in the violence and confusion.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:22.780
This time there is a happy ending.

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:37.580
As summer progresses, even more of the Southern Ocean is gradually opening up.

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:51.420
The summer melt as it moves south arrives first at the Antarctic Peninsula,

00:33:51.420 --> 00:34:01.940
the most northern tip of the continent. As the ice retreats, a dramatic 500-mile long

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:06.180
coastline is revealed for the first time in seven months.

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:18.900
This new seascape is home to one of the most numerous mammals on the planet.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:39.540
Crab eater seals. Over 15 million live here amongst the drifting ice flows. They owe their

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:49.220
existence here to living organisms so small you might hardly notice them. The underside of the

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:57.180
sea ice is stained by algae. These microscopic plants support the most important Antarctic

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:10.140
creatures of all, krill. They have been grazing on the algal layer throughout the winter. As the

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:20.180
ice melts, more of the algae are released into the water. Both algae and krill flourish in the

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:32.740
summer sun. The krill collect the algae in the sieve-like basket formed by the interlocking

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:48.540
hairs between their front legs. There are 300 million tons of krill in the Southern Ocean,

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:53.900
with a greater combined weight than that of any other animal on the planet.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:36:20.180
Almost all the animals of the Antarctic depend on krill, including its giants. Humpback whales.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:25.980
They drive the krill to the surface and then strain it from the water with the

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:49.780
baleen sieves in their mouths. Humpbacks often feed in teams, so the overspill from one huge

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:53.060
mouth can be collected by another just behind.

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:34.180
The abundance of krill attracts other visitors to the peninsula in the summer.

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:50.940
Antarctic minke whales. Their pointed heads and short dorsal fins give them speed and endurance.

00:37:50.940 --> 00:38:17.020
And they need both. There are other whales here too. Killers. This is an extended family

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:26.740
of mothers and their young. And they're male with a huge dorsal fin almost two meters high.

00:38:26.740 --> 00:38:49.900
Alone minke whale. It's just what this group of killer whales are looking for.

00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:59.300
Working as a team, as they have done for decades, they fan out across the strait in search of their

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:28.140
quarry. And they found it. The minke races away, pursued by outriders on each flank.

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:54.380
Terrified, the minke heads for the shore. It's so desperate to escape, it almost beaches

00:39:54.380 --> 00:40:21.020
itself. It makes a desperate break for freedom. Two hours and 20 miles later, the minke is

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:29.380
still alive and swimming strongly. Its only real defense is its endurance. But the killers

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:49.100
work as a team with fresh ones replacing the outriders in relays. And as the minke tires,

00:40:49.100 --> 00:41:09.140
the battering and the biting begins. Sea birds are attracted by the smell of fresh blood

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:33.140
rising from the water. The killers try to flip the minke over. If they can manage to

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:44.860
keep its blowhole underwater, it will drown. One forces the minke's whole body down below

00:41:44.860 --> 00:42:12.420
the surface. And then the final strike. The team drag the minke under for the last time

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:35.740
and the hunters finally can feed. As the summer advances, the most southerly stretches of

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:51.180
the Antarctic Ocean are released from the ice. A daily penguins have been feeding in

00:42:51.180 --> 00:43:08.700
the open ocean. The most southerly of all penguins, they're returning to the Antarctic

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:25.060
continent with stomachs full of crew.

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:48.740
Oh no! Huh Thank you God we didn't

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:49.740
Ah!

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:08.780
The sea ice has finally retreated all the way back to the beach,

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:13.300
so the walk back to the colony is as short as it will ever be.

00:44:14.260 --> 00:44:16.700
Crucial for the half million penguins here,

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:20.860
who are hurrying to raise their chicks before the freeze returns.

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:27.740
Most of the pairs have two chicks to care for.

00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:35.860
Each chick requires nearly 30kg of food before it's fully grown.

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:42.540
Most of their catches krill,

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:48.580
and Adelie penguins consume a staggering one and a half million tons of it a year.

00:44:56.660 --> 00:45:00.460
There are no land-based predators in Antarctica.

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:10.420
The threat here comes from the skies.

00:45:10.420 --> 00:45:27.440
China's higher-class

00:45:27.440 --> 00:45:52.440
An unguarded chick is an easy catch.

00:45:52.440 --> 00:46:12.440
Nothing can save the chick now.

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:35.440
But now the parents can focus all their attention on the second.

00:46:52.440 --> 00:47:00.440
Only half of these chicks will survive to adulthood.

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:07.440
But there are thousands more parents with young in this huge colony.

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:36.440
Parents that cannot rest, for their young must be fit and strong if they are to survive the dangers that autumn will bring.

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:48.440
Next time, autumn and the sun starts to set on the frozen planet.

00:47:48.440 --> 00:48:16.440
The race is on to finish breeding before the big freeze.

00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:26.440
In summer, the frozen oceans melt and the polar animals disperse to feed amongst the broken ice.

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:38.440
So even finding them in this vast expanse of sea and drifting ice floes wouldn't be easy.

00:48:38.440 --> 00:48:53.440
In the Arctic, the aim was to get close to a polar bear family and film them in this fast melting world.

00:48:53.440 --> 00:49:05.440
Firstly, the team had to use a ship capable of breaking through the pack ice around the 2000 mile long coastline of Svalbard.

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:15.440
Producer Miles Barton and cameraman Ted Giffords are checking out fjords known to be frequented by bears.

00:49:15.440 --> 00:49:18.440
We've just seen a bear walking around in front of a glacier over there.

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:24.440
We've just arrived, so this is a kind of a test more than anything, but we're going to go and see if we can film it.

00:49:24.440 --> 00:49:36.440
We're seeing how smoothly we can make this operation work.

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:41.440
Stay on channel 6, can you give me directions to the bear please?

00:49:41.440 --> 00:49:51.440
The plan is that the team's stabilised aerial camera, now fitted to the speedboat, will give perfectly stable shots of the bear swimming.

00:49:51.440 --> 00:49:53.440
Spin this around, we'll lose that thing.

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:55.440
There's a fellow over here, yeah?

00:49:55.440 --> 00:49:57.440
Oh yeah, got him, he's got him.

00:49:57.440 --> 00:49:59.440
Can you shoot it?

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:06.440
Weeeee!

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:08.440
Look at the lights!

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:10.440
Nice sparkle, nice liquid look.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:11.440
Let him go.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:15.440
OK.

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:24.440
After their initial success, the weather takes a turn for the worse.

00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:31.440
For five days, there are no more polar bears to be found.

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:39.440
The captain decides to take a break and parks up in the pack ice.

00:50:39.440 --> 00:50:48.440
After all that fruitless searching by the crew, a bear visits them.

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:58.440
A large and inquisitive adult male.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:04.440
So the team decides to follow him.

00:51:04.440 --> 00:51:12.440
The open water between boat and bear means the crew can safely film from surprisingly close range without disturbing him.

00:51:12.440 --> 00:51:14.440
That's nice.

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:18.440
Look.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:24.440
The bear is so relaxed he even begins to hunt right alongside them.

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:27.440
He's looking for a seal inside.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.440
He's just completely ignoring this.

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:30.440
Just hold it there.

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:33.440
Just hold it there for a minute.

00:51:33.440 --> 00:51:35.440
He's looking to climb up.

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:40.440
Ready?

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:46.440
That's a big bear.

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:49.440
That's pretty good, pretty good.

00:51:49.440 --> 00:51:53.440
The closest we've been to a polar bear so far.

00:51:53.440 --> 00:52:01.440
Despite this remarkably close encounter, the team still wants to film a family of bears.

00:52:01.440 --> 00:52:06.440
Please film after two cubs to starboard side.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:09.440
There's good news on the radio.

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:10.440
Oh, there's a cub.

00:52:10.440 --> 00:52:13.440
Yeah, I've seen the cubs.

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:14.440
They're looking straight at us.

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:16.440
Look at that.

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:17.440
That's amazing.

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:26.440
As the mother looks distinctly hungry, they will need to keep at least 15 feet of open water between her and the boat.

00:52:26.440 --> 00:52:29.440
They're having a good look at us.

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:30.440
Oh, look at them.

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:31.440
Look at the cubs.

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:38.440
Yeah.

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:43.440
Jason, just go when you need to.

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:44.440
Oh, she's young.

00:52:44.440 --> 00:52:47.440
Bloody hell.

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:55.440
Other bears are always desperate for food for their cubs, so the crew could be the perfect supper.

00:52:55.440 --> 00:52:56.440
She's going in.

00:52:56.440 --> 00:52:59.440
I guess she's going to go in the water with her.

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:02.440
The 15-foot gap was just enough.

00:53:02.440 --> 00:53:05.440
That was amazing.

00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:08.440
Hands are freezing.

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:11.440
We have to go.

00:53:11.440 --> 00:53:13.440
OK.

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:16.440
Do you want to get close-ups of the cubs?

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:18.440
Yeah, that's cute.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:20.440
She's going in the water.

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:21.440
OK.

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:24.440
And then stay with the cubs.

00:53:24.440 --> 00:53:28.440
Clear the cubs for a nice splash.

00:53:28.440 --> 00:53:29.440
Go on, cubs.

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:32.440
Yeah.

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:33.440
Well done.

00:53:33.440 --> 00:53:35.440
Well done.

00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:38.440
Great stuff.

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:43.440
She looked like she wanted to get in the boat once or twice.

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:49.440
Sometimes it's quite shocking to actually look up from the monitor and realise they're about 15 feet away from you.

00:53:49.440 --> 00:53:55.440
But a lovely family group.

00:53:55.440 --> 00:54:00.440
The boat-based camera has helped to get remarkably close shots of the bear family.

00:54:00.440 --> 00:54:04.440
But how would it cope in the more extreme conditions of the Southern Ocean?

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:17.440
Another frozen planet team headed south.

00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:27.440
The plan is to use the same camera system to film the hunting strategy of the most spectacular marine predators in Antarctica.

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:31.440
Killer whales.

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:37.440
The team enlisted the help of scientists Bob Pitman and John Durbin.

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:44.440
They had put satellite tags on the whales and so could locate them for the camera team.

00:54:44.440 --> 00:54:52.440
Doug Allen operates the camera rig from the wheelhouse while Doug Anderson films from the deck.

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:54.440
Yeah, that's it.

00:54:54.440 --> 00:54:56.440
It just surfaced right with them.

00:54:56.440 --> 00:55:02.440
OK, there's a killer whale at 11 o'clock, about 200 metres heading towards those two seals on the floor.

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:03.440
OK, good.

00:55:03.440 --> 00:55:05.440
This could be really good.

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:12.440
At last, having tracked the group for several days, the whales look as if they're about to hunt.

00:55:12.440 --> 00:55:14.440
Yeah, they're going to go in for it.

00:55:17.440 --> 00:55:19.440
There it goes.

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:24.440
Four whales here.

00:55:24.440 --> 00:55:28.440
Two adult females, a juvenile and a big adult male.

00:55:28.440 --> 00:55:34.440
Swimming in formation, the killers create a wave to wash the seal off the flow.

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:36.440
So pretty intense.

00:55:36.440 --> 00:55:38.440
Oh, another wave.

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:40.440
Yes, wave.

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:42.440
Wow.

00:55:42.440 --> 00:55:44.440
Yeah, he's in the water.

00:55:44.440 --> 00:55:53.440
Having successfully filmed several hunts with the boat-based cameras from above, the team are keen to see what's happening underwater.

00:55:54.440 --> 00:55:56.440
Only one way to find out.

00:55:56.440 --> 00:56:06.440
Time to launch the dinghy.

00:56:06.440 --> 00:56:22.440
Doug Anderson approaches the seal and the killers with an underwater camera mounted on a pole.

00:56:22.440 --> 00:56:31.440
For the first time, the team can see how the whales create the killer wave.

00:56:31.440 --> 00:56:36.440
There it is.

00:56:36.440 --> 00:56:42.440
The underwater camera also reveals surprisingly cautious behaviour by the whales.

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:47.440
They seem wary of being bitten by the much smaller seal.

00:56:47.440 --> 00:56:51.440
And they even blow bubbles to confuse it.

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:53.440
They're so cautious with the seals.

00:56:53.440 --> 00:57:01.440
At the end of the day, the seal's got a big mouth full of teeth and these whales just don't take risks.

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:14.440
Having dealt with the seal, the whales turn their attention to the dinghy.

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:25.440
First, they get right up close for a better look at Doug.

00:57:25.440 --> 00:57:34.440
Then they line up to create the kind of wave that washed the seal off the flow.

00:57:34.440 --> 00:57:45.440
For a moment, the crew get an uncomfortably close seal's eye view of this remarkable hunting strategy.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:52.440
Look. See that, Bob? The jubilees are making waves.

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:59.440
It's a great feeling being there, being so close to the behaviour, actually having the water coming into the boat.

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:04.440
From our point of view, it's all about trying to get the feeling of being with these whales.

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:08.440
And with these whales, there's no bother with that because they're so confident.

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:12.440
They just want to be all around you and checking you out.

00:58:12.440 --> 00:58:15.440
I can't imagine that I'm going to have another experience like this in my career.

00:58:15.440 --> 00:58:22.440
These come along rarely and you just feel happy and lucky when they do come.

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:29.440
You just don't get better days than that.

00:58:52.440 --> 00:59:02.440
